An angel investor is evaluating the potential of investing in two start-ups, A and B, recommended by their mentor. The investor has a certain amount of capital, \( C \), to allocate between these two start-ups. The investor's goal is to maximize the expected return on investment (ROI) over a period of 5 years. The ROI for each start-up is modeled as a continuous random variable with the following probability density functions (PDFs):

For start-up A:
\[ f_A(x) = \begin{cases} 
\frac{3}{4}(2 - x^2) & \text{if } 0 \leq x \leq 1, \\
0 & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases} \]

For start-up B:
\[ f_B(x) = \begin{cases} 
3(1 - x^2) & \text{if } 0 \leq x \leq 1, \\
0 & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases} \]

1. Determine the expected ROI for each start-up, A and B, over the 5-year period.
2. If the investor decides to allocate \( \alpha \times C \) to start-up A and \( (1 - \alpha) \times C \) to start-up B (where \( 0 \leq \alpha \leq 1 \)), formulate an optimization problem to maximize the expected total ROI. Identify the optimal value of \( \alpha \).
